<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Fixed Stars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 964</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Fixed Stars is an astronomical treatise written around 964 by the Persian astronomer Abd al-Rahman al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> and dedicated to the Buyid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>. It revised the star catalogue of Ptolemy's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a> with new observations of positions and brightness, illustrated each constellation and recorded Arabic star names. Widely copied in the Islamic world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, it contains an early description of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andromeda-galaxy">Andromeda Galaxy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-fixed-stars.jpg" alt="Book of Fixed Stars" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abd al-Rahman al-Sufi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unknown,_Iraq_-_The_Book_of_Fixed_Stars_-_Google_Art_Project_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
